“Stay in the plenum.”

“I need the camera card.”

“You need to remain outside the room until I open the door.”

He lowered one foot to the booth floor.

Diane moved between him and the tripod. “Your entry is against instruction and enters the primary camera frame from an unrecorded edge.”

“My camera records it.”

“Your camera lacks the synchronized clock and began after the test.”

He paused with one knee on the hatch sill.

Abby’s voice reached them faintly from the dimmer room. “Evan, come back.”

Diane crossed to the booth door. The deadbolt lever turned without resistance from inside. She drew it back and opened the door.

The paper seal tore across her initials.

Abby stood on the landing with the main camera raised. Relief crossed her face before she steadied the camera.

“Time,” Diane said.

“Five twenty-nine and eleven seconds.”

“Record the intact lower half of the seal, the door hardware, and the assigned key.”

Diane inserted B-seventeen from inside. The cylinder turned smoothly.

Evan climbed through the hatch behind her.

“The key works now,” he said.

“Door pressure changed after the deadbolt retracted. Cylinder alignment changed with it.”

“Or something held the lock.”

“The first explanation has a mechanism.”

“The second has a chair.”

Abby moved onto the threshold. “Where is the wet key?”

“Secured.”

Evan pointed toward Diane’s pocket. “She removed it from the scene.”

“After documenting its position,” Diane said. “The key remains with facilities until identification.”

Abby kept the camera on Diane. “Show the pocket without opening it.”

Diane turned sideways. The zipper lay closed. A damp patch had already begun to darken the blue work shirt around it.

All three fell quiet at the sight.

Water had passed through the lens cloth.

Diane felt no cold against her skin. The pocket lining separated the key from her body, though the wet mark continued to widen.

“Possible retained water in the cloth,” she said.

Evan looked at the dry chair seat. Only the narrow key-shaped outline remained.

“Primary camera,” Diane said. “We retrieve the card under continuous coverage.”

She approached the tripod. The display showed the booth from the same low-light angle as the monitor. A warning icon indicated dropped frames during the period of exposure pulsing. Recording continued.

Diane stopped the file, stated the time, and removed the memory card. Abby filmed the card entering a labeled evidence envelope. Evan filmed with the auxiliary camera until Diane held out her hand.

“That camera stays with Abby.”

“It contains the crawlspace record.”

“Which belongs in the same evidence copy.”

He surrendered it.

The chair remained beneath the glassless opening. Diane left it in place. Moving it again would create another undocumented route and another opportunity for someone to substitute a story for the record.

They returned to the stage together.

The monitor still displayed the final frozen frame after the booth camera stopped: Diane’s hand reaching toward the tripod, her body occupying the right side of the image, the door open behind her.

Abby connected the primary card to the laptop in read-only mode. Diane stood close enough to watch every action. Evan stayed across the road case with his hands visible.

“Review only the entry,” Diane said. “No filters, trimming, gain changes, or labels.”

Abby opened the original file and moved to five twenty-eight.

The booth sat empty. Chair at center. Door closed. Camera steady.

The rear edge of the equipment rack shifted when Diane opened the maintenance hatch. A vertical band of lighter pixels appeared beside it. Her shoe entered frame. Her leg followed. Then her full body lowered into the room.

Abby paused.

“Continue,” Diane said.

The file advanced one frame at a time.

Diane cleared the hatch and stepped toward the chair.

At the booth door, a pale human-like outline remained in the image.

It occupied the narrow space between door and console, nowhere near the maintenance hatch. Its height approximated Diane’s. One shoulder seemed turned toward the glassless opening. The shape persisted while Diane’s recorded body crossed the room.

One frame.

Two.

Three.

Four.

On the fifth, the outline stretched sideways and broke into square blocks.

The image collapsed into compression noise.

When it recovered, the door stood empty and Diane knelt beside the wet key.

# **Scene 10 — Source Track**

The following morning, the media room smelled of warm plastic, stale coffee, and the dry paper dust released whenever the old ventilation unit started. Diane sat at the end of a laminate table with the unidentified key sealed in a clear evidence envelope beside her clipboard. Water had evaporated from the brass during the night, leaving a pale mineral edge around the bow and a darker line inside the ward. The envelope carried the date, the booth location, Abby’s camera time, Diane’s initials, and the signatures of the assistant principal and the security aide who had taken custody of the building after the students left. No tag number matched the key. No master-ring inventory listed its shape. Its cuts were shallow and old, worn smooth where a thumb had pressed it through years of use or through one long period of handling that had ended before anyone in the room could name.

Across the table, Evan had opened his laptop between two external drives. Abby sat beside him with her camera card still inside a write-protected reader. The assistant principal occupied a rolling chair near the door, jacket folded over one arm, face set in the cautious expression administrators used when an event contained students, staff, locked rooms, and several possible versions of liability. Ron had declined the meeting. His written statement lay beneath Diane’s clipboard in three short paragraphs: breathing at the basement junction, request to end the session, departure from the auditorium before the booth test. He had signed each page in block letters.

Diane preferred his account to every recording in the room.

Ron had defined exactly where his account ended.

Evan connected the final drive and projected his editing timeline onto the wall. Colored tracks stacked across the screen: Abby’s continuous camera, the booth camera, the receiver cache, the recorder files, radio audio, room tone, Evan’s narration, and a music bed labeled LOW DRONE. The marker names already treated disputed moments as facts.

KEY RESPONSE.

WAYNE VOICE.

FIGURE CROSSES BOOTH.

LOCK CHANGES.

CHAIR MANIFESTS.

Diane read them in silence while the assistant principal adjusted her glasses.

“This is a working edit,” Evan said. “The labels are internal.”

“Internal labels guide every cut after them,” Diane said.

“They help me organize.”

“They tell you what the material means before you compare it.”

Evan touched the trackpad. The first frame showed Bellweather High under evening clouds. A low electronic note entered beneath his voice.

“Bellweather High has stood for more than sixty years, but staff say the building keeps more than records—”

Diane reached across the table and pressed the space bar.

The narration stopped.

“No,” she said.

Evan leaned back. “You agreed to review it.”

“I agreed to review evidence and discuss a limited student documentary. I gave no agreement for this sentence.”

“It is an opening.”

“It is a conclusion wearing an opening’s clothes.”

The assistant principal raised one hand. “Let us separate production choices from access questions. Evan, show the raw files first.”

Evan closed the preview window. He controlled his irritation, but every small action showed it: the firm click on folders, the quick expansion of waveforms, the refusal to look at Diane while he aligned the tracks. Abby watched him with the exhausted patience of someone who had spent the night copying files twice, calculating checksums, and discovering that technical preservation could become another argument about ownership.

They reviewed each source in chronological order. The basement cadence survived on Abby’s camera but changed direction and weight across devices. Wayne’s phrase appeared complete on one recording and broken on another. The chair’s movement remained outside every useful frame. RECOVERED\_07 lacked a source timestamp. Diane logged creation times, transfers, codec changes, and gaps while Abby identified camera positions and the assistant principal separated reportable fact from inference.

By ten-thirty, fear had become file paths, automatic gain, dropped frames, and incomplete access records. The review weakened every individual claim without producing one explanation that covered them all.

The result should have comforted Diane.

With each contradiction, Diane felt less certain that she understood the building.

At eleven-eight, Evan opened the booth-camera file.

The green-gray room appeared on the wall. The pale image change crossed beside the door, broke into blocks, and vanished when exposure settled.

Evan’s earlier microphone check entered through the monitor path. Wayne’s phrase followed. On the booth camera’s original audio, however, the phrase was thinner than it had sounded live. The first two words dissolved into broadband noise. The final word retained only a hard beginning and a long vowel.

“Nothing clear,” Diane said.

Evan increased volume.

The assistant principal heard “a joke.” Abby heard “I’m close.” Evan heard the complete phrase he had supplied before the test.

Diane heard Wayne.

She recognized the cadence despite lacking proof. His upward pull on here seemed absent from the booth file, then returned when Evan replayed the fragment, then vanished under headphones. Diane removed them and placed them on the table. Chair wheels scraped, and the ventilation unit pushed dry air through the room.

“Show Abby’s source track at the same time,” she said.

Evan opened the continuous camera file and aligned its timecode against the booth feed. Abby’s microphone had recorded the monitor speaker from center stage, the auditorium’s return, Diane’s voice naming conditions, Evan’s breathing, and the thin electrical buzz of the road-case power strip. They watched the pale form cross. Evan’s recorded question came through the monitor.

Then static.

Then, beneath the sound of Diane’s hand closing around the road case, a low sequence arrived that Diane heard as complete.

Come here, I have a joke.

No one spoke.

Evan enlarged the waveform. Four low peaks stood beneath the monitor hiss. He muted the booth track.

The same low sequence remained.

He muted the receiver cache.

The same low sequence remained.

He muted the laptop’s system-audio capture.

The same low sequence remained.

Only Abby’s camera contained the complete sequence Diane recognized.

Diane looked toward Abby. “Your microphone was open the entire time?”

“Continuous from the hallway through the booth entry. No pause.”

“Any wireless receiver?”

“Internal stereo mic. Manual level. Airplane mode.”

“External speaker near you?”

“The monitor, eight feet forward. Laptop beside it.”

Evan unmuted the booth camera and receiver. The aligned tracks produced the confused, layered version they remembered from the auditorium. When he isolated the direct booth source again, the phrase collapsed into noise.

The assistant principal said, “Could Abby’s camera have recorded someone in the auditorium?”

“All three of us were in frame,” Abby said. “Main doors and stage-left entrance were visible. The booth opening stayed visible until we moved up the aisle.”

“A person behind the camera?”

Abby rotated the source video to the reflective road-case lid. Her own outline occupied the left edge. Empty stage floor extended behind her. No fourth figure appeared.

Evan scrubbed backward. “The phrase is on her track because her camera heard the monitor more clearly.”

“The monitor received audio from the booth,” Diane said. “The booth source lacks the complete phrase.”

“The receiver altered it.”

“Then the receiver output should appear on the system-audio capture.”

“Unless the laptop routing changed.”

“Show the routing log.”

He opened it. According to the available session log, the receiver had maintained one connected camera and one audio channel. The log showed no second input or playback command. The thumbnail cache contained images only.

